---
/**
 * The product page.
 *
 * Longer and more technical than the home page, because the second visit is
 * usually made by the person who will have to defend the choice internally.
 * The last section lists what is specified but not yet built. It is there
 * deliberately: this is a product sold on the reliability of its record, and a
 * roadmap presented as a feature list is the exact failure the product exists
 * to prevent.
 */
import Marketing from "../layouts/Marketing.astro";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";

const partyRoles = [
  ["convener", "Opened the Circle, holds the closure right, receives the packet."],
  ["principal", "The party the work is ultimately for."],
  ["contractor", "Engaged to deliver a defined part of it."],
  ["subcontractor", "Engaged by a contractor, a level down."],
  ["advisor", "Contributes expertise without carrying delivery."],
  ["observer", "Present on the record without contributing."],
];

const trustAxes = [
  {
    axis: "Origin",
    values: ["authenticated upload", "external submission", "system import", "manual statement"],
    body: "Where the item came from and under whose credentials. This is knowable, so it is recorded.",
  },
  {
    axis: "Integrity",
    values: ["intact", "hash mismatch", "superseded"],
    body: "Whether the stored bytes still match the hash taken at ingest. Also knowable, and checked rather than asserted.",
  },
  {
    axis: "Review",
    values: ["unreviewed", "in review", "accepted", "contested"],
    body: "What a human has said about it. Never inferred from the other two, and never set by an agent.",
  },
];

const notYet = [
  ["URL capture", "A source URL is recorded against an evidence item, but there is no fetcher, no HTML snapshot and no content hash for a captured page."],
  ["Notification transport", "Mentions are recorded and surfaced in the application. Nothing leaves the browser yet — no email, no push. Until it does, a deadline is state somebody has to remember to go and look at."],
  ["The action executor", "Proposals and approvals are recorded correctly. The runner that takes an approved action and performs it, honouring retries and the idempotency key, is not written."],
  ["Party-scoped resource permissions", "“Contributors see only their branch of the tree” is a real design question rather than a column. Permissions today are Circle-wide plus per-resource overrides."],
  ["Handoff", "Reassigning everything a departing person owns is still a hunt rather than one action."],
  ["Packet coverage", "The export packet ends at the original object set. It does not yet read the goal tree, the comment threads or the action ledger."],
  ["Live transcription", "Shipped as a provider interface. With no provider configured, transcripts are recorded as skipped rather than left pending. The path is untested against a live provider."],
];
---

<Marketing
  title="Product"
  description="How Circle works: parties rather than guests, evidence with three separate trust axes, claims that cite exact locations, a goal tree with responsible parties, and agents bounded by a declared mandate."
  path="/product"
>
  <div class="shell">
    <header class="max-w-[54ch]">
      <p class="eyebrow">Product</p>
      <h1 class="editorial mt-4 text-[clamp(2.2rem,5vw,3.4rem)] text-[var(--ink)]">
        Eight objects, <em>and the rules between them.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
        Circle is deliberately small. A Circle holds parties, evidence, claims,
        decisions, commitments, goals, comments and agent actions — and nothing
        else. What makes it useful is not the object list; it is what the system
        refuses to let those objects do to each other.
      </p>
    </header>
  </div>

  {/* --------------------------------------------------------------- parties */}
  <section class="shell mt-20 md:mt-28">
    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.85fr_1.15fr] lg:gap-14">
      <div>
        <p class="fieldno">01 · PARTIES</p>
        <h2 class="editorial mt-3 text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.6vw,2.4rem)] text-[var(--ink)]">
          Nobody is a guest.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
          Most tools model the outside world as “external users” — a lesser class
          of person visiting somebody else's workspace. That is the wrong shape for
          work between companies, because responsibility belongs to an organisation
          before it belongs to a person.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
          Each participating organisation gets a party record with a commercial
          position. A party can be named before its organisation exists on the
          platform, because counterparties are written into a plan long before
          anyone from them signs in. People leave projects; the company still owes
          the deliverable, and reassigning a person must not silently move
          liability between companies.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
          A party role is a commercial position, not a permission set. Permissions
          come from each member's Circle role. The party's job is to make the export
          packet read correctly — “contractor” is worth more in a record than
          “external”.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card overflow-hidden self-start">
        <header class="rule-b px-5 py-3">
          <h3 class="label">Commercial position</h3>
        </header>
        <dl class="register">
          {
            partyRoles.map(([role, meaning]) => (
              <div class="grid grid-cols-[8rem_1fr] gap-4 px-5 py-3.5">
                <dt class="mono text-[0.8125rem] text-[var(--ink)]">{role}</dt>
                <dd class="text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
                  {meaning}
                </dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* -------------------------------------------------------------- evidence */}
  <section class="mt-20 rule-t bg-[var(--paper-inset)] md:mt-28">
    <div class="shell py-20">
      <div class="max-w-[54ch]">
        <p class="fieldno">02 · EVIDENCE</p>
        <h2 class="editorial mt-3 text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.6vw,2.4rem)] text-[var(--ink)]">
          Three axes. Three vocabularies. <em>No green tick.</em>
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
          A single “verified” badge collapses three unrelated questions into one
          answer, and the answer is always slightly false. Circle keeps them apart,
          and shows all three, always. A document is routinely
          <span class="mono text-[0.8125rem] text-[var(--ink)]"
            >authenticated upload · intact · unreviewed</span
          > — and being able to say exactly that is the point.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
        {
          trustAxes.map((axis) => (
            <article class="card flex h-full flex-col p-5">
              <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">{axis.axis}</h3>
              <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
                {axis.body}
              </p>
              <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
                {axis.values.map((value) => (
                  <li class="stamp mono" style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
                    {value}
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <article class="card p-5">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
            Originals are never overwritten
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            A replacement is a new version pointing back at what it supersedes, so a
            citation made in March still resolves to the exact bytes it cited in
            March. Superseding an item does not quietly rewrite the history of every
            claim that rested on it.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card derived-panel p-5">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620]" style="color: var(--derived)">
            Interpretations are separate objects
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            Every OCR pass, transcript, thumbnail and agent summary is a derived
            artifact recording which model, which prompt version and which source
            produced it. Derived content is indigo and labelled everywhere it
            appears, so it cannot be mistaken for evidence at a glance.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- claims */}
  <section class="shell mt-20 md:mt-28">
    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.15fr_0.85fr] lg:gap-14">
      <div>
        <p class="fieldno">03 · CLAIMS AND CITATIONS</p>
        <h2 class="editorial mt-3 text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.6vw,2.4rem)] text-[var(--ink)]">
          A citation that cannot resolve <em>is refused, not stored.</em>
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
          A claim points at a precise location inside a specific version of a
          specific item — a page in a PDF, a range in a named sheet, an offset in
          seconds into a recording. The locator is validated against the media type
          at the moment the claim is filed, so a citation nobody can follow never
          enters the record in the first place.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
          Human claims enter as <span class="mono text-[0.8125rem]">attested</span>.
          Agent claims enter as <span class="mono text-[0.8125rem]">derived</span>.
          Neither is ever silently promoted; moving a claim's status is an action by
          a named person, on the record.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card overflow-hidden self-start">
        <header class="rule-b px-5 py-3">
          <h3 class="label">Locator, by media type</h3>
        </header>
        <dl class="register">
          <div class="px-5 py-3.5">
            <dt class="text-[0.8125rem] text-[var(--ink)]">Document</dt>
            <dd class="mono mt-1 text-[0.75rem] text-[var(--ink-faint)]">
              &#123;"page": 4&#125;
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="px-5 py-3.5">
            <dt class="text-[0.8125rem] text-[var(--ink)]">Spreadsheet</dt>
            <dd class="mono mt-1 text-[0.75rem] text-[var(--ink-faint)]">
              &#123;"sheet": "Load Schedule", "range": "C2:D2"&#125;
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="px-5 py-3.5">
            <dt class="text-[0.8125rem] text-[var(--ink)]">Audio or video</dt>
            <dd class="mono mt-1 text-[0.75rem] text-[var(--ink-faint)]">
              &#123;"start_seconds": 133&#125;
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ goal tree */}
  <section class="mt-20 rule-t bg-[var(--paper-inset)] md:mt-28">
    <div class="shell py-20">
      <div class="max-w-[54ch]">
        <p class="fieldno">04 · THE GOAL TREE</p>
        <h2 class="editorial mt-3 text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.6vw,2.4rem)] text-[var(--ink)]">
          A date that can move silently <em>carries no weight.</em>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
        <article class="card p-5">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
            Sub-goals are goals
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            Each carries its own acceptance condition and its own responsible party,
            which is exactly what happens when work is subcontracted a level down.
            Claims, decisions and commitments can each hang off a node — or off
            none, so a Circle that never builds a tree behaves as it always did.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card p-5">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
            A parent cannot claim 80%
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            Reported progress stays distinct from derived progress. A parent ignores
            its own stored figure and averages its children, because a parent at 80%
            over sub-goals at 20% is the precise failure a status field exists to
            prevent.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card p-5">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
            Every movement of a date
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            Recorded with a reason, who moved it, and — where the move lands on
            another party — whether that party agreed. It is its own table rather
            than a query over the log, because a slipped date is the single most
            common thing inter-company projects end up arguing about.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* --------------------------------------------------------- conversation */}
  <section class="shell mt-20 md:mt-28">
    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.85fr_1.15fr] lg:gap-14">
      <div>
        <p class="fieldno">05 · CONVERSATION</p>
        <h2 class="editorial mt-3 text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.6vw,2.4rem)] text-[var(--ink)]">
          There is no general channel, <em>on purpose.</em>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5">
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
          Threads hang off a goal, a claim, a decision, a commitment or an evidence
          item. There is no room to drift into — and that is the design, not an
          omission. Once a general channel exists, the substance migrates into it
          and the structured record decays into something someone updates
          afterwards out of duty.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
          Earlier versions of this product let a person speak only while changing a
          state: a review comment, an approval note, a commitment update. That is
          why the real conversation left for email — there was no way to ask a
          question without committing to an action. Now a comment may carry an
          action or carry nothing, and an object's history reads as one conversation
          rather than two parallel records.
        </p>

        <div class="card overflow-hidden">
          <dl class="register">
            <div class="px-5 py-4">
              <dt class="display text-[0.9375rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
                Visibility defaults to your own party
              </dt>
              <dd class="mt-1.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
                The opposite default from evidence, and deliberately so. Evidence is
                what the Circle exists to pool. A contractor working out its own
                position in front of the client is how a shared workspace stops
                being used at all. A party-scoped thread is invisible to everyone
                outside that party — the convener included.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="px-5 py-4">
              <dt class="display text-[0.9375rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
                Export inclusion is per comment
              </dt>
              <dd class="mt-1.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
                A comment that carried a state change is part of the decision record
                and is always in the packet. Plain discussion is out unless someone
                marks it for the record. If every aside were discoverable in a
                dispute, people would self-censor into uselessness and the feature
                would be worth nothing.
              </dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- agents */}
  <section id="agents" class="mt-20 rule-t bg-[var(--paper-inset)] md:mt-28">
    <div class="shell py-20">
      <div class="max-w-[56ch]">
        <p class="fieldno" style="color: var(--derived)">06 · AGENTS</p>
        <h2 class="editorial mt-3 text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.6vw,2.4rem)] text-[var(--ink)]">
          The verification machinery <em>is what makes execution sellable.</em>
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
          Claims, decisions and an audit chain look like paperwork right up until
          software starts acting on your behalf in someone else's project. Two
          companies that do not fully trust each other will not allow it unless
          every action is attributable, bounded by a declared mandate, and
          refusable. That is the whole argument.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <article class="card p-5">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
            The rule the schema enforces
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            An agent proposes, and a side effect needs a human holding the authority
            of the party that bears it. An agent never acts for “the Circle”; it acts
            for a party, and that party carries the consequence. It cannot approve
            anything — including its own proposal.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card p-5">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
            Mode is checked before the tool list
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            Execution mode is an outer bound evaluated in the access gate before the
            declared actions, so a blueprint asking for more than its mode allows
            gets nothing. Dropping an agent to <span class="mono">read_only</span> is
            one column, and it stops everything that agent could do without editing a
            single tool.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card p-5">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
            Classified by consequence
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            Tools are classified by what they do to the world — none, circle write,
            external read, external write, financial — rather than by what they are
            called. A blueprint may raise a tool's approval bar and never lower it,
            so an author cannot mark their own payment tool as needing nobody, and
            the rules hold for tools nobody has written yet.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card p-5">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
            Bring your own agent, and your own keys
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            A party's agent runs under that party and its own credentials; Circle
            never holds the raw secret. A counterparty asked to admit it is shown a
            key fingerprint and approves that specific key, not a name anyone could
            type. Admission is a decision on the record, not a setting in a panel.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card p-5 lg:col-span-2">
          <h3 class="display text-[1rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">
            The ledger is written before anything is attempted
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[70ch] text-[0.875rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
            An action row is created at <span class="mono">proposed</span>, so a
            refused or failed action leaves exactly the same trail as a successful
            one — the same reason a run records its retrieval manifest before the
            model is called. The tool key and its side effect are stored flat, so
            the ledger still reads correctly after a blueprint is edited or a tool
            removed. Approval reuses the decision machinery rather than inventing a
            second one, and closure disables every agent in the Circle outright.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- notyet */}
  <section class="shell mt-20 md:mt-28">
    <div class="max-w-[54ch]">
      <p class="fieldno">07 · WHAT IS NOT BUILT YET</p>
      <h2 class="editorial mt-3 text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.6vw,2.4rem)] text-[var(--ink)]">
        Named honestly, <em>because the schema implies them.</em>
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">
        This list is on the marketing site rather than in a footnote of a pilot
        agreement. A product sold on the reliability of its record cannot start by
        overstating itself, and you will find all of this in the first week
        anyway.
      </p>
    </div>

    <dl class="register mt-10 rule-t">
      {
        notYet.map(([label, detail]) => (
          <div class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-2 py-6 md:grid-cols-[16rem_1fr]">
            <dt class="display text-[0.9375rem] font-[620] text-[var(--ink)]">{label}</dt>
            <dd class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-muted)]">{detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p class="mt-8 max-w-[68ch] text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-faint)]">
      Also deliberately absent, and not planned: native chat, a freeform canvas, a
      general agent marketplace, connectors into Drive, SharePoint, email or issue
      trackers, verifiable credentials, blockchain anchoring, and enterprise SSO or
      SCIM. Each is a decision with a reason rather than a gap.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- cta */}
  <section class="mt-24 rule-t sheet">
    <div class="shell py-24 text-center">
      <h2 class="editorial mx-auto max-w-[22ch] text-[clamp(1.9rem,4.4vw,2.9rem)] text-[var(--ink)]">
        The fastest way to judge this <em>is on a real project.</em>
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Request a pilot</a>
        <a href="/security" class="btn btn-quiet">Security overview</a>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-7 text-[0.8125rem] text-[var(--ink-faint)]">
        {SITE.stage.line}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Marketing>
